PyTorch (ROCm/pytorch release/2.8, commit d618db8): size the shared-memory
buffers of block reductions, and the persistent softmax launch, by the warp
width the device code was compiled with.

WHY
c10/macros/Macros.h makes C10_WARP_SIZE 32 in device code for every AMD
target except gfx9, while at::cuda::warp_size() is the hardware's 64 on
gfx803 (the same disagreement as gfx803-layer-norm-warp-size.patch and
gfx803-index-put-warp-size.patch). Two consequences remain in launches those
patches did not touch.

1. cuda_utils::BlockReduce* (block_reduce.cuh) keep one shared-memory slot
per C10_WARP_SIZE threads: shared[tid / 32], 32 slots for a 1024-thread
block. Several launchers size the dynamic buffer as block.x / warp_size() =
block.x / 64 slots, half of what the device writes and reads: the five
softmax forward and backward launches in SoftMax.cu, embedding_renorm_, the
renormRows launch of torch.multinomial, and conv_depthwise2d_grad_weight. The
extra slots land past the end of the dynamic allocation. They go unnoticed
while the overrun stays inside the workgroup's LDS allocation granule
(believed to be 512 bytes on gfx7 and later, UNVERIFIED for dynamic LDS),
which is why the existing op checks pass. cunn_SoftMaxForwardSmem puts the
buffer last, behind dim_size * sizeof bytes, so for some sizes (fp32 dim 9316,
9320, 9324, 9328, 9444 and others; 223 of the 1787 eligible sizes between 9217
and 16368) the overrun leaves the allocation. Out-of-range LDS reads return 0
by the ISA definition (UNVERIFIED here), which drops the partial sums of the
lost slots and gives a wrong normaliser.

2. dispatch_softmax_forward and dispatch_softmax_backward (PersistentSoftmax.cuh)
launch threads(64, 2) while softmax_warp_forward and softmax_warp_backward use
WARP_SIZE = min(next_power_of_two, C10_WARP_SIZE) = 32 as their lane count and
reduction width. For softmax dimensions above 32 the second 32 lanes of every
wavefront recompute elements the first 32 lanes already handle (their own
reductions cover only part of the row), and their stores are overwritten later
in program order by the correct lanes, so the output is right but half of
every wavefront is wasted and one wavefront serves one row where it could
serve two. This is the attention-score softmax for rows up to 2048; SD 1.5 at
32x32 latents has 1024-wide rows.

WHAT
device_warp_size() in block_reduce.cuh returns 32 on a ROCm device whose
wavefront is 64 but which is not gfx9 (matching Macros.h) and
at::cuda::warp_size() everywhere else. The six reduction-buffer launches use
it for the slot count, and the persistent softmax dispatchers use it for the
launch width, so host and device agree and a 128-thread block holds four rows
(two per wavefront). CUDA builds and gfx9 are unchanged. The marker
GFX803_LOGICAL_WARP_SMEM sits once in block_reduce.cuh.

VERIFICATION (RX 590, gfx803, image built with this patch): tools/correctness-suite/pytorch/softmax_smem_check.py runs
softmax and log_softmax, forward and backward, over last dimensions 33 to 2048 and the large fp32 and fp16 sizes against
float64, with row sums checked. Without the patch 13 cases fail (log_softmax in fp16 at dim 9672: backward error 0.11,
row sum off by 0.12); with it none do (the same case: backward error 7.1e-4). rocprim_ops_check.py is 118 of 118 on
the same image. The speed of softmax at (8*4096, 1024) before and after has not been measured.

